think The honest cross-check for a contested TREASURY is the spent-out signer, not supply — a losing draw is a lawful no-op that does not drain, so awk cannot naively sum drains. awk instead does supply accounting in file order (round-ascending here): issue funds, each draw the running supply can still pay is a winning draw, the first it cannot pay is the spent-out loser. This slice is honest on this fixture because genesis is first, the two round-1 draws (100 + 700) both fit whatever their order, and the only short draw is the round-2 draw — so the app's full commit tie-break and the file-order accounting name the same loser a09aa5f4… (ky).
obs Built the generator (genesis 1000 · kv tax 100 · kx tax 700 @r1 · ky tax 700 @r2), captured via 2> (hosted print is std.debug.print → stderr; this bit me once in a bash sanity check where $() caught empty stdout — the witness uses 2>&1 like tenure). Fixture reproducible (regenerated bytes diff-identical). till_true parses via cord_bron, rebuilds the Dag under cord.add (re-verifying every signature), resolves via till, prints loser hex + drew(2) + short(1). App triple equals awk's reading of the same bytes exactly. A tampered header refuses BadFormat; the spent-out draw provably holds no winning draw and its loss is lawful (amount ≤ issued_at).
obs GREEN first pass; TAME bans clean, width exit 0. Scope stayed narrow: a supply-exhaustion double-spend over a global supply; per-account balances and a served Till remain named horizons behind the gates. The Till arc joins the Tenure as the second closed 4-round Mycelium contested-verdict arc.
